import{_ as t,o,c as s,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.7R1OEGYz.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Integrated browser","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"browser.md","filePath":"browser.md","lastUpdated":1793867610000}'),a={name:"browser.md"};function n(i,e,d,h,l,c){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="integrated-browser" tabindex="-1">Integrated browser <a class="header-anchor" href="#integrated-browser" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Integrated browser&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A session can have its own visible Chromium. The agent starts it when it needs one, drives it through built-in browser tools, and you watch — and, if you may, take over — the same desktop beside the chat. Until the agent asks for it, a session has no browser and uses no browser CPU or memory.</p><p><strong>Edition:</strong> Community · <strong>Who:</strong> session owner and Collaborators control it; Viewers watch; operators enable it and size it</p><h2 id="starting-a-browser" tabindex="-1">Starting a browser <a class="header-anchor" href="#starting-a-browser" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Starting a browser&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There is no start button. Ask the agent to open a page, and it calls <code>browser_start</code> itself. While the browser pod comes up, the pane says <em>Preparing browser…</em> and <em>The agent requested a browser. It appears here automatically.</em> Once it runs, the pane opens beside the conversation and the session bar shows a <strong>Browser</strong> button.</p><p>Each session has at most one browser. It keeps running until the agent calls <code>browser_stop</code>, or the session is paused, finishes or is deleted.</p><h3 id="the-agent-s-browser-tools" tabindex="-1">The agent&#39;s browser tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-agent-s-browser-tools" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The agent&#39;s browser tools&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode sessions get the built-in <code>agenthub_browser</code> MCP server; a user MCP configuration cannot replace it. The Cursor and OpenClaw runtime images do not ship it, so those sessions have no integrated browser.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>browser_start</code>, <code>browser_status</code>, <code>browser_stop</code></td><td>Start, inspect and stop the session&#39;s browser</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser_navigate</code></td><td>Open a URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser_snapshot</code></td><td>Read the visible text and element references of the page</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser_click</code>, <code>browser_type</code></td><td>Click an element, type into a field</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser_screenshot</code></td><td>Capture the page</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser_tabs</code></td><td>List, select, open or close tabs</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Before each action the agent&#39;s tab is brought to the front, so you always see the page it is working in.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Autonomous sessions</p><p>An autonomous session&#39;s allow list has to cover the browser tools, for example <code>mcp__agenthub_browser__*</code> — unless auto-approve is on. See <a href="/docs/auto-approve">Auto approve</a>.</p></div><h2 id="watching-and-controlling-the-browser" tabindex="-1">Watching and controlling the browser <a class="header-anchor" href="#watching-and-controlling-the-browser" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Watching and controlling the browser&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The pane streams the browser&#39;s desktop. Its status line tells you which access you have:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>Live · shared control</em></td><td>Owner, Collaborators, Collaborator share links — mouse and keyboard reach the page</td></tr><tr><td><em>Live · view only</em></td><td>Viewers and Viewer share links — the server refuses their input</td></tr></tbody></table><p>You and the agent share one desktop, so you can step in to sign in somewhere, solve a prompt the agent cannot, or point at something. Access is checked again every two seconds: a revoked link or a Collaborator downgraded to Viewer loses control of an open view.</p><p>If the connection drops, the pane reconnects on its own; Chromium keeps running meanwhile.</p><h3 id="copy-and-paste" tabindex="-1">Copy and paste <a class="header-anchor" href="#copy-and-paste" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Copy and paste&quot;">​</a></h3><p>With control access, <strong>Ctrl/Cmd+C</strong>, <strong>X</strong> and <strong>V</strong> over the browser pane copy and paste plain text between your own computer and the remote page. Only text is carried, up to 256 Ki characters; password fields give nothing on copy. A copy made by one person never reaches others watching the same browser. If your browser refuses clipboard access, the pane says so.</p><h2 id="arranging-the-pane" tabindex="-1">Arranging the pane <a class="header-anchor" href="#arranging-the-pane" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Arranging the pane&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Resize</strong>: drag the seam between browser and conversation (25 to 75 %).</li><li><strong>Hide</strong>: the <strong>×</strong> on the BROWSER tab, or Esc, closes the pane — <em>the agent keeps it</em>. Reopen it with <strong>Browser</strong> in the session bar.</li><li><strong>Phone</strong>: Files, Browser and Agent become tabs.</li></ul><h3 id="pop-out-into-its-own-window" tabindex="-1">Pop out into its own window <a class="header-anchor" href="#pop-out-into-its-own-window" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pop out into its own window&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Click <strong>Pop out</strong>, or drag the <strong>BROWSER</strong> tab onto <em>Drop here to pop out Browser</em> or release it outside the app window. The browser opens in a separate window — for example on a second screen — with mouse, keyboard and clipboard working there.</p><p>While it is popped out, the pane in the session is hidden and the conversation takes the width; if Files is open, Files is shown. Return it with <strong>Bring back</strong> in the session, <strong>Bring back to session</strong> in the window, or by closing the window. Closing the session view also closes its pop-out. If your browser blocks pop-ups, the pane asks you to allow them and stays docked.</p><p>Not tested: where the window lands on a physical second monitor.</p><h2 id="cookies-are-kept-per-session" tabindex="-1">Cookies are kept per session <a class="header-anchor" href="#cookies-are-kept-per-session" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cookies are kept per session&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With object storage configured, the browser&#39;s <strong>cookies</strong> are saved periodically and restored when the browser starts again — after <code>browser_stop</code>, a pause or a resume. Only cookies: no history, cache, downloads, local storage, saved passwords or browser profile. Deleting the session deletes them; duplicating a session does not copy them. Without object storage every browser starts with a clean cookie jar.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">The agent sees what the browser sees</p><p>The agent can read and act on every page and cookie in its browser. Sign in to accounts there only in sessions whose prompts, repositories, MCP servers and collaborators you trust.</p></div><h2 id="previewing-what-the-agent-builds" tabindex="-1">Previewing what the agent builds <a class="header-anchor" href="#previewing-what-the-agent-builds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Previewing what the agent builds&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The browser can open a dev server running in the session itself — see <a href="/docs/preview">Previewing a page the agent is building</a>.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>One window: pages that try to open a popup window get a tab instead. A window that still opens separately is reloaded as a tab, so a form POST result and its opener are lost.</li><li>No GPU. WebGL works through software rendering; heavy 3D pages are slow with the default one-CPU limit.</li><li>Network: the browser reaches DNS, HTTP and HTTPS, plus any ports the operator added.</li></ul><h2 id="for-operators" tabindex="-1">For operators <a class="header-anchor" href="#for-operators" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;For operators&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The browser is on by default (<code>browser.enabled: true</code>) and costs nothing until used. Setting it to <code>false</code> removes the browser tools from every session.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>browser.resources</code></td><td>250m / 512Mi requested, 1 CPU / 2Gi limit</td><td>Per browser pod</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser.screen</code></td><td><code>2560x1600</code></td><td>Desktop size</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser.startupTimeoutSeconds</code></td><td><code>90</code></td><td>How long a start may take</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser.cookieCheckpointSeconds</code></td><td><code>60</code></td><td>Cookie save interval</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser.extraEgressPorts</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>Destination ports beyond 80/443</td></tr><tr><td><code>browser.previewPorts</code></td><td><code>[3000, 4173, 5173, 8000, 8080]</code></td><td>Session ports the browser may open</td></tr></tbody></table><p>More in <a href="/docs/chart-values">Chart values</a>.</p>',35)])])}const b=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{w as __pageData,b as default};
